18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L20",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 20, 1864","place":"North of Atlanta, Georgia","result":"United States victory","facts":[{"label":"Confederate attacks covered","value":"Maney's, Loring's, and Walthall's divisions"},{"label":"United States defenders","value":"Ward's, Geary's, and Williams's XX Corps divisions"},{"label":"Casualties stated in the episode","value":"About 1,900 U.S.; 2,500 Confederate","note":"Published totals vary by source."},{"label":"31st Mississippi","value":"164 casualties among 215 men","note":"A reported casualty rate of about 76 percent."}],"maps":[{"title":"The battlefield today","caption":"The central and western fighting described in this episode occurred around Tanyard Creek, Collier Road, and the ground west toward Howell Mill Road.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.443%2C33.788%2C-84.368%2C33.838&layer=mapnik&marker=33.8105%2C-84.4032"},{"title":"Troop positions and Confederate attacks","caption":"Blue marks the U.S. line. Red marks the Confederate line and attack directions. This episode follows the attacks against Ward, Geary, and Williams from the center toward the U.S. right.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BATTLE_OF_PEACHTREE_CREEK.jpg","image":"/images/peachtree-tactical-map.jpg","alt":"U.S. Army map of the Battle of Peachtree Creek showing United States positions in blue and Confederate positions and attacks in red"},{"title":"Atlanta campaign operations","caption":"An 1875 U.S. Army Corps of Engineers map showing Atlanta, Decatur, roads, waterways, fortifications, and military operations from July 19 through August 26, 1864.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447303/","image":"/images/peachtree-operations-map.jpg","alt":"1875 military operations map of Atlanta and the surrounding region"},{"title":"U.S. positions at Peachtree Creek","caption":"A wartime manuscript map showing U.S. camps and troop positions north of Atlanta around Peachtree Creek.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2007626736/","image":"/images/peachtree-union-positions.jpg","alt":"Wartime manuscript map of United States troop positions at Peachtree Creek"}],"moments":[{"time":"4:00","audioTime":240,"title":"Bate and Walker failed to turn the U.S. left","detail":"The episode begins by reviewing the earlier attacks. Bate's division missed the U.S. line, and Walker's division was stopped by John Newton's prepared position."},{"time":"5:16","audioTime":316,"title":"Maney's division stopped short of the U.S. center","detail":"George Maney's four Tennessee brigades advanced into thick woods, halted, and sent skirmishers forward. They did not make the major attack that Hood's plan required."},{"time":"7:27","audioTime":447,"title":"Featherston and Scott advanced toward Tanyard Creek","detail":"Two brigades from William Loring's division moved through woods and broken ground, crossed an open field divided by Tanyard Creek, and attacked William Ward's XX Corps division."},{"time":"10:32","audioTime":632,"title":"Ward's brigade commanders moved to the ridge","detail":"John Coburn and Benjamin Harrison recognized that Ward had left the high ground ahead unoccupied. When the Confederate attack began, Harrison advanced his brigade and Coburn and James Wood also moved their brigades forward."},{"time":"11:30","audioTime":690,"title":"The U.S. center stopped the Confederate attack","detail":"Harrison, Coburn, and Wood closed the gap between Newton and Geary. Their brigades took the ridge and forced Featherston's and Scott's troops back toward the woods."},{"time":"14:25","audioTime":865,"title":"Walthall's brigades attacked the U.S. right","detail":"Daniel Reynolds's and Edward O'Neal's brigades attacked parts of Alpheus Williams's and John Geary's divisions near Howell Mill Road. The defenders had warning because the battle had already moved across the line from east to west."},{"time":"15:51","audioTime":951,"title":"The 27th Indiana reinforced Knipe","detail":"Joseph Knipe called for support after Reynolds threatened his right flank. The 27th Indiana moved across an open field and fired from the edge of a gully, helping hold the line."},{"time":"20:06","audioTime":1206,"title":"Geary and Williams held the U.S. right","detail":"The Confederate brigades failed to break either division. Geary's and Williams's commands suffered much of the U.S. loss but retained their positions."},{"time":"21:24","audioTime":1284,"title":"Firing ended after about three hours","detail":"The fighting diminished around 7 p.m. and had stopped by about 8 p.m. The Confederate army withdrew from the attacks without breaking the Army of the Cumberland."},{"time":"22:44","audioTime":1364,"title":"Losses were concentrated in heavily engaged units","detail":"The episode gives totals of about 1,900 U.S. and 2,500 Confederate casualties. Featherston's brigade lost about 615 men, and the 31st Mississippi reported 164 casualties among 215 men engaged."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"George H. Thomas","command":"Army of the Cumberland","subordinates":"Army engaged at Peachtree Creek"},{"commander":"Joseph Hooker","command":"XX Corps","subordinates":"Alpheus Williams, John Geary, William Ward"},{"commander":"William T. Ward","command":"Third Division, XX Corps","subordinates":"Benjamin Harrison, John Coburn, James Wood"},{"commander":"Alpheus S. Williams","command":"First Division, XX Corps","subordinates":"Joseph Knipe, Thomas Ruger, James Robinson"},{"commander":"John W. Geary","command":"Second Division, XX Corps","subordinates":"Charles Candy, Patrick Jones, Thomas Ireland"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"John B. Hood","command":"Army of Tennessee","subordinates":"Ordered the attack"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Hardee's Corps","subordinates":"George Maney commanded the division discussed here"},{"commander":"Alexander P. Stewart","command":"Stewart's Corps","subordinates":"William Loring and Edward Walthall"},{"commander":"William W. Loring","command":"Loring's Division","subordinates":"Winfield Scott Featherston, Thomas M. Scott"},{"commander":"Edward C. Walthall","command":"Walthall's Division","subordinates":"Daniel Reynolds, Edward O'Neal"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Benjamin Harrison","role":"Led a brigade in Ward's division onto the ridge; later became the 23rd U.S. president","side":"United States","image":"/images/benjamin-harrison.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/96524264/"},{"name":"John W. Geary","role":"Commanded the XX Corps division holding part of the U.S. right","side":"United States","image":"/images/john-geary.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666423/"},{"name":"John Coburn","role":"Led a brigade in Ward's division and pressed for occupying the ridge before the attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Knipe","role":"Called for reinforcements when Reynolds threatened his brigade's right flank","side":"United States"},{"name":"Edmund R. Brown","role":"27th Indiana sergeant whose account of Knipe and the regiment is read in the episode","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joel D. Murphree","role":"57th Alabama sergeant whose letter describes the regiment’s losses and Bailey Talbot’s disappearance","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Jabez L. Drake","side":"Confederacy","role":"Colonel of the 33rd Mississippi Infantry","outcome":"Killed while leading the regiment in Featherston’s brigade. He and men from the regiment were buried on the battlefield."},{"name":"Bailey M. Talbot","side":"Confederacy","role":"Captain in the 57th Alabama Infantry","outcome":"Killed during the attack. Joel Murphree’s letter describes the initial uncertainty over whether Talbot had been killed, wounded, or captured."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/peachtree-union-graves.jpg","alt":"Temporary graves of United States soldiers on the Peachtree Creek battlefield in 1864","caption":"Temporary U.S. graves close to where the soldiers fell at Peachtree Creek, photographed in 1864.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PeachtreeCreek1864.jpg"},{"image":"/images/peachtree-battle-drawing.jpg","alt":"Adolph Metzner watercolor drawing of the Battle of Peachtree Creek made in 1864","caption":"Adolph Metzner, an officer in the 32nd Indiana, drew this view of the battle in 1864.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646908/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 538 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/08/16/civil-war-podcast-episode-538/"},{"label":"Episode 538 transcript index","url":"https://podcastrex.com/shows/the-civil-war-reconstruction/538-the-atlanta-campaign-peach-tree-creek"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History campaign study","url":"https://history.army.mil/Publications/Publications-Catalog/The-Atlanta-and-Savannah-Campaigns/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ga016"},{"label":"National Park Service Confederate order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/confederate-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Benjamin Harrison after-action report","url":"https://ironbrigader.com/2017/07/16/future-president-benjamin-harrisons-report-brigade-battle-peachtree-creek/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Atlanta operations map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447303/"},{"label":"Library of Congress U.S. positions map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2007626736/"},{"label":"Library of Congress John Geary portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666423/"},{"label":"Library of Congress battlefield drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646908/"},{"label":"Wikimedia Commons battlefield graves photograph","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PeachtreeCreek1864.jpg"}]}}]}]
